    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Smeared shale thicknesses within a fault zone were measured from fifteen wells in the Niger delta, using wireline log data. Subsurface fault and stratigraphic data were used to derive throw values across the fault under consideration. The results show a decrease in shale smear thickness, given similar-sized original shale units, with increasing fault throw when a single and isolated shale source is involved in the smearing process. This result is in agreement with behavior documented from previous work carried out in the field in other parts of the world. However, for smearing developed from multiple shale source units, smear thicknesses do not show a particular relationship to fault throw distribution. Important results of the study are: (1) the ability to measure thicknesses of smeared shale from common wireline log data; (2) confirmation of results from surface studies for a single isolated shale layer smeared along segments of a fault; and (3) presentation of a new methodology to analyze composite cases which appear to differ from that of a single isolated case.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
